@@s001 Data structures: which to choose and what it costs
@@s002 ora:bit that changes in this increment,dato:bit unchanged,ok:result
@@s003 Binary counter: an increment flips the bits from the bottom until it finds a 0, which becomes 1. <b>Cost of an increment</b>: how many bits change.
@@s004 0000 → 0001: 1 bit changes.
@@s005 0001 → 0010: 2 bits change (1 resets to 0, 0 becomes 1).
@@s006 0011 → 0100: 3 bits. Only one changes "always", the lowest one; the others change rarely.
@@s007 In 8 increments (0 → 8) bit 0 changes 8 times, bit 1 four times, bit 2 twice, bit 3 once: in total <b>15 changes</b> for 8 operations, that is about 2 per operation.
@@s008 Data structures: which to choose and what it costs
@@s009 At the exam the data structures serve to justify the time. Choose the structure by looking at the operation you repeat most often, and multiply its cost by how many times you repeat it.
