@@s032 Amortised cost
@@s033 An <b>amortised</b> cost is a bound on the <i>sum</i> of the costs of a sequence of operations, divided by the number of operations. A single operation can cost a lot, but the sequence does not. Example: the binary counter. Each increment changes many bits, but in 8 increments the total changes are 15: about 2 per operation, therefore <code>O(1)</code> amortised.
@@s034 The binary counter: how many bits change
@@s035 Dijkstra and the queue
@@s036 Dijkstra with binary heap: <code>O(m log n)</code>. With an unsorted array: <code>O(n²)</code>, good for dense graphs. With a Fibonacci heap: <code>O(m + n log n)</code>, more theoretical. The exam accepts the binary heap as the standard answer.
